name w1-red-flag
time 2022-08-16T17:00:00Z
carry units ground stores
stop_on never

# CAMPAIGN W1 - RED FLAG / NELLIS, the training ladder.
# Spec, anchor and sources: doc/campaigns/w1-red-flag.md. Format: doc/missions/campaign.md.
#
# WHY THIS CAMPAIGN WAS UNBLOCKED. Its spec listed W1 as blocked on the aggressors: the catalogue's
# adversary rows are ALPHA and A15 forbids an ALPHA row from being scored in a gun fight. W1's own point
# dissolves that. At Nellis the "MiG-29" is an F-16 of the 64th Aggressor Squadron in an adversary scheme
# [T4]; in FlightBox the aggressor is the REAL mig29 module. THE CAMPAIGN THEREFORE NEEDS NO CATALOGUE ROW
# AT ALL - all eleven files fly `f16` and `mig29` and nothing else, which is the same discipline W3, W4 and
# O3 demonstrated, and A15 is untouched. The substitution's DIRECTION is stated in every mission header:
# the FlightBox aggressor carries the R-73, the KOLS and a helmet cue the real aggressor F-16 never had, so
# Red is materially STRONGER than the historical opposing force. A Blue result that still goes well says
# something stronger than a Nellis debrief did; one that goes badly says nothing about the 64th.
#
# THE ANCHOR IS [T1] AS OF THIS ROUND. The 414th Combat Training Squadron's own fact sheet has been carried
# in doc/campaigns/PROGRESS.md as unreadable ("HTTP 403 to automated retrieval") since run 1. It is READ
# here through the Wayback Machine's capture of the identical URL (web.archive.org/web/20240116184035/,
# "current as of October 2022"), which is the path that lifted O2's two CIA documents, and it moves five
# claims from [T3]/[T4] to [T1]: the exercise's purpose and form; its founding in 1975 under Lt Col Richard
# "Moody" Suter at Gen Robert J. Dixon's direction; THE TEN-MISSION RATIONALE VERBATIM ("if a pilot
# survived his first 10 combat missions, his probability of survival for remaining missions increased
# substantially... Red Flag was designed to expose each 'Blue' force pilot to their first 10 'combat
# missions'"); the five Blue mission types (offensive counter air, SEAD, combat search and rescue, dynamic
# targeting, defensive counter air); and the NTTR target inventory the ground half of every file is drawn
# from (mock airfields, vehicle convoys, tanks, parked aircraft, bunkered defensive positions, missile
# sites). It also gives the aggressors' own job as "a scalable threat presentation... which aids in
# achieving the desired learning outcomes for each mission" - THE LADDER, at [T1].
# What it does NOT give is a single package size: the numbers are cumulative since 1975 (30,268 aircraft,
# 423,248 sorties, 529,722 personnel) and there is no air tasking order in it. EVERY FORCE SIZE IN THIS
# CAMPAIGN IS THEREFORE STILL [SET], and PROGRESS.md's "package sizes unsourced" row stands.
# One temporal disagreement is carried rather than resolved: the spec has the aggressors consolidated under
# the 57th Adversary Tactics Group, formed 1 July 2005 [T4]; the 2022 fact sheet aligns the Red force
# threats under the 57th OPERATIONS Group [T1]. Both are stated; neither is preferred.
#
# THE ARENA is one east-west lane at 37.30 N from -116.58 (the aggressors' entry) to -115.52 (Blue's, the
# Nellis side), inside the spec's own NTTR box (37.0-37.6 N, 115.5-116.6 W [T4]). It is 93.9 km long, and
# THAT IS THE ARENA'S MOST IMPORTANT PROPERTY: the APG-68's gate against a Fulcrum-sized target is 100.0 km
# (doc/duels.md Knowledge 1), so the range is shorter than Blue's radar reach and no Blue jet on it ever
# flies a search phase. The N019's gate against an F-16 is 50.0 km, i.e. 53 % of the lane. Every campaign
# result here is a result about a SHORT arena, and doc/duels.md's own numbers were taken over 111 km.
# GROUND: `--elev const`, 0 m range floor, and passing it explicitly is a PRECONDITION and not a
# convention - fb-gym defaults to `swiss` when the baked DEM is on disk and validates declared spawn
# altitudes against the resolved ground (O3's warning). Every altitude in every W1 file is therefore a
# height above a flat plane and not a chart ASL figure; the real NTTR floor is ~1.2-1.5 km and C4 means
# there would be nothing to mask behind it anyway.
#
# THE CLOCK. 2022-08-16T17:00Z is 10:00 local at Nellis (PDT = UTC-7) and is what nine of the ten sorties
# fly under; w1-10-graduation.fbm declares its own, 2022-08-17T07:00Z = local midnight, and that one
# `time` line is the ONLY thing that makes it a night ride. Both instants are [SET] - no exercise date is
# sourced anywhere - and the runner logs the computed sun elevation at each MISSION_START so the claim is
# checkable in the output.
#
# `carry units ground stores` - ALL THREE, AND `units` IS THE ONE THAT IS CARRIED ON PURPOSE. O4 narrowed
# `units` away with a good reason (a DACT kill is a call; both jets land at Laage). RED FLAG IS THE
# OPPOSITE CASE AND THE SPEC SAYS SO: its cast table lists "Range control / kill removal" as *"not a unit -
# a campaign-layer function (C0)"*. Kill removal is a documented range procedure, and dropping a destroyed
# unit from the next mission is exactly what doc/missions/campaign.md section 3 does. W1 is therefore the
# campaign in which the carry performs the anchor's own function rather than modelling attrition.
#
# WHERE THE CARRY LANDS, AND IT IS EXACTLY ONE PLACE (campaign rule 2). Sorties 09 and 10 are ONE range
# period flown by ONE set of airframes: `nvblu1..4` and `nvagr1..4` fly both, and the four range objects
# (`nvcvy1`, `nvcvy2`, `nvpark`, `nvbnkr`) stand in both. Nothing bombs them in 09, so all four enter the
# graduation ride intact and the aggressors' magazines enter it as 09 left them. THE OTHER EIGHT SORTIES
# CARRY FRESH CALLSIGNS, ground objects included, and that is not decoration: the store book is keyed by
# callsign, so a controlled variant that shared one with its control would inherit its expenditure and stop
# being a control (campaign rule 1). The two controlled pairs are 03/04 (one more aircraft a side) and
# 07/08 (one token: the lead's radar), and neither overlaps the chain (campaign rule 6).
#
# WHAT IS NOT MODELLED, priced rather than hidden. There is no AAA and no surface-to-air missile on this
# range (the aggressor air-defence squadrons the fact sheet names have no counterpart here - C1 is closed
# for the eastern rows, and no Western AAA row exists at all), no tanker (C5) though every Red Flag ride
# tanks, no AEW (C6) though the exercise's whole picture comes from one, no radio (C18), no terrain masking
# (C4), no strafing (C11), and no time-of-day switch other than the `time` line. The consequence for the
# CLAIM is stated once here: W1 measures the AIR-TO-AIR and AIR-TO-GROUND halves of a Red Flag ride and
# nothing about the threat array that decides its altitudes.
#
# THE TEN RUNGS, EACH ADDING EXACTLY ONE DEGREE OF FREEDOM OVER THE ONE BEFORE, and each mission's own
# header carries its binding reading rule (doc/missions/INDEX.md rule 5) - this list is the index only:
#   01 merge          1v1 close combat, 8 km. Does a NEUTRAL merge convert on the first pass?
#   02 two-v-one      + one Blue aircraft. With one target for two shooters, does the sort double up?
#   03 bvr-single     + the range axis: 93.9 km, one a side. At what multiple of Rtr does he fire?
#   04 bvr-pair       + one aircraft a side. Does the cooperative sort hold two distinct targets?
#   05 defensive-ca   + the ground objective, on RED's side. Can a CAP deny a target it stands over?
#   06 strike-escort  + a second Blue role. Does the escort stay tied to the strikers?
#   07 emcon          + the sensor axis: both sides one sensor down. What does total silence buy?
#   08 degraded       07 with ONE token changed. Is a radar-less wingman a shooter or a passenger?
#   09 lfe-four       + two aircraft a side and the weather fixture. How far does the sort's churn rise?
#   10 graduation     + night, two flights, and a ground job for BOTH sides. Does the package hold?
#
# READING RULE FOR THE CAMPAIGN. The exit code is the WORST step's code and it is expected to be non-zero:
# most rungs are two-sided engagements whose objectives cannot all conclude while anybody is alive, and
# each header says its verdict is its telemetry. The campaign's verdict is the `campaign MISSION_RESULT`,
# `campaign CARRY`, `campaign ATTRITION` and `campaign EXPENDED` lines plus the per-rung readings, never
# the code. A campaign fingerprint is comparable only within ONE ground base and ONE sky
# (doc/missions/campaign.md section 5): compare only against runs whose campaign-summary.txt records
# `elev const` and `time 2022-08-16T17:00:00Z`.
#
# AND ONE MEASUREMENT W1 OWES THE OTHER NINE. A ladder whose rungs all return the same outcome is not a
# ladder, so this campaign is checked against doc/doctrine-evolution.md section 4.2's saturation gate with
# its own numbers - modal outcome class <= 60 %, >= 3 of 9 doctrine levers moving the outcome class, >= 6
# geometries per arena. The result is in doc/campaigns/w1-red-flag.md section State.

mission ../missions/w1-01-merge.fbm
mission ../missions/w1-02-two-v-one.fbm
mission ../missions/w1-03-bvr-single.fbm
mission ../missions/w1-04-bvr-pair.fbm
mission ../missions/w1-05-defensive-ca.fbm
mission ../missions/w1-06-strike-escort.fbm
mission ../missions/w1-07-emcon.fbm
mission ../missions/w1-08-degraded.fbm
mission ../missions/w1-09-lfe-four.fbm
mission ../missions/w1-10-graduation.fbm
